.TH "rbc::TRACKGRAPH" 3tcl 0\&.8\&.1 "rbc" "Upgraded Tcl/Tk9\&.0-ready RBC package"
.SH NAME
rbc::TRACKGRAPH - Commands in namespace rbc::TRACKGRAPH
.SH "TRACKGRAPH LAYOUT AND TRACKGRAPH COMMANDS"
.PP
This page documents widgets created by \fI::rbc::trackgraph\fP and their named-track operations\&.
TRACKGRAPH is a fictional documentation prefix for widgetPath track, not a runtime command\&.
For example, TRACKGRAPH create voltage -yaxes volts means \&.g track create voltage -yaxes volts\&.
The procedures below describe that component API; they are not Tcl wrappers to invoke directly\&.
.PP
Common widget commands and configuration are described in \fI::rbc::GRAPHINST\fP\&. Use \fI::rbc::AXIS\fP for axes,
\fI::rbc::LINEELEMENT\fP and \fI::rbc::STRIPELEMENT\fP for traces, \fI::rbc::MARKER\fP for annotations, and
\fI::rbc::LEGEND\fP for global and track-local legends\&. Toolbar integration is described in \fI::rbc::graphtoolbar\fP\&.
.SH "STACKED TRACKS"
.PP
rbc::trackgraph is one native graph window containing named vertical plot regions\&. It reuses the graph's
line and strip elements, renderers and SVG, PDF and PostScript exporters\&. It does not create child graphs\&.
The axis-region approach is also used by BLT's -stackaxes feature; RBC tracks explicitly group one or
more Y axes and their elements into a row\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
rbc::trackgraph \&.g -width 800 -height 500
\&.g axis create voltageY -title Voltage
\&.g axis create currentY -title Current
\&.g track create voltage -weight 2 -yaxes voltageY
\&.g track create current -weight 1 -yaxes currentY
\&.g line create voltageTrace -track voltage -mapy voltageY -xdata time -ydata voltage
\&.g strip create currentTrace -track current -mapy currentY -xdata time -ydata current
pack \&.g -fill both -expand yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
Tracks initially appear in creation order from top to bottom\&. Use track order to change their order without
recreating axes, elements or markers\&. Every track receives the same horizontal plotting bounds\&.
.PP
The common left margin accommodates the widest set of track Y axes, including titles and tick labels\&. X axes
remain widget-wide: elements select them with -mapx, and ordinary xaxis use and x2axis use control their
display\&. Independent X axes need not have matching tick positions\&. By default, tracks inherit the graph's
\fI::rbc::GRID\fP settings: grid -mapx selects the shared X axis, and a nonempty grid -mapy enables grid lines
from each track's first Y axis\&. An empty axis selection disables that direction\&. See \fIgrid\fP for independent
grid settings within each track\&.
.PP
.nf
┌──────────────┬─────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Track option │ Default │ Meaning                                                                                     │
├──────────────┼─────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ -weight      │ 1\&.0     │ Positive finite share of remaining layout space after track minima are allocated; at most   │
│              │         │ 1000000\&.                                                                                    │
│ -minheight   │ 40      │ Positive minimum requested plot height, or width when inverted, in Tk screen-distance       │
│              │         │ units\&.                                                                                      │
│ -height      │ 160     │ Requested plot height in scroll layout, or width when inverted; from 1 to 1000000 pixels,   │
│              │         │ with Tk screen-distance units accepted\&. The effective size is at least -minheight\&. Ignored  │
│              │         │ in fit layout\&.                                                                              │
│ -yaxes       │ empty   │ List of existing Y axes assigned exclusively to this track and displayed in its left margin │
│              │         │ (bottom when inverted)\&.                                                                     │
└──────────────┴─────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

.fi
.SS "SCROLLING TRACKS AND THEIR DATA"
.PP
The default -tracklayout fit divides the available plot space using -weight and -minheight, as before\&.
With -tracklayout scroll, each track retains max(-height, -minheight) pixels and adjacent tracks have
a 12-pixel gap\&. Content may extend beyond the visible viewport\&. Track weights are ignored in this mode\&.
Scrolling moves tracks vertically, or horizontally with -invertxy yes; shared X axes remain fixed\&.
Tracks do not change their data ranges or rescale when partially visible\&. Drawing and picking use only
the visible part\&. bbox returns that visible rectangle; a fully hidden track has zero size along the
scrolling direction\&. Transforms still use the full track geometry and may return offscreen coordinates\&.
.PP
Use \fIview\fP for the track list and \fIyview\fP for local data scrolling\&. They are independent:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g configure -tracklayout scroll -trackscrollcommand {\&.tracks set}
\&.g track configure voltage -height 280
ttk::scrollbar \&.tracks -orient vertical -command {\&.g track view}
\&.g axis configure voltageY -scrollmin -10 -scrollmax 10 -scrollcommand {\&.volts set}
ttk::scrollbar \&.volts -orient vertical -command {\&.g track yview voltage -mapy voltageY}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Position the scrollbar widgets with ordinary Tk geometry management\&. For an inverted graph, use horizontal
scrollbars\&. Each track may have a separate local scrollbar, and each of its Y axes may have one too\&.
Existing axis -scrollmin, -scrollmax, -scrollincrement, and -scrollcommand settings apply to local
scrolling; the graph's -trackscrollcommand receives the track-list viewport's first and last fractions\&.
Use finite axis scroll limits when the full data range is not a useful scroll region\&.
.PP
Separator dragging in scroll layout changes the preceding track's requested -height and the total
content extent\&. It leaves the next track's size and both weights unchanged\&. Escape restores the original
size\&. In fit layout, dragging continues to redistribute adjacent weights\&. A change to the list viewport,
orientation or layout mode cancels active separator/toolbar gestures rather than reusing stale pixels\&.
.PP
SVG, PDF and PostScript export the viewport at the current scroll offset, with track sizes expressed in
output pixels; a differently sized export can include more or fewer tracks\&. Export does not change the
screen scroll position or notify its scrollbar\&. Local axes, legends, grids and ordinary markers are
clipped to the viewport\&. Window markers retain the existing partial-child-clipping limitations described
in \fI::rbc::MARKER\fP; fully offscreen track windows are hidden\&.
demos/trackgraph1\&.tcl includes a track-list scrollbar and separate local Y scrollbars\&.
.PP
Assign axes with track configure -yaxes, rather than yaxis use\&. An axis may belong to only one track\&.
Elements must specify both -track and a -mapy axis belonging to that track\&. Change both options in one
element configure call to move an element\&. An axis in use by an element cannot be detached, and an axis owned
by a track cannot be deleted until detached\&. These ownership checks include markers, even hidden markers or
markers with empty coordinates, and Y axes selected by independently configured track grids\&. Hiding an axis
hides its decorations, not its track\&. Lists and RBC vectors retain their ordinary ownership and notification behaviour\&.
.PP
Track transforms default to shared axis x and the track's first Y axis\&. Use -mapx or -mapy to select
alternatives\&. Ordinary graph-level transform and invtransform are ambiguous in a trackgraph and report
an error; individual axis transform and axis invtransform operations remain available\&.
.PP
The layout fits the available height\&. After minimum heights, spare space is distributed by weight\&.
Tracks have a 12-pixel gap, reduced in very small windows\&. If minimum heights do not fit, they are scaled
proportionally to avoid forcing the window larger\&. Resizing and export recompute the layout\&. Elements and
closest-point searches are restricted to their own track; normal, descending and logarithmic Y scales work
independently\&. Horizontal zoom or pan is performed through the shared X axes; vertical navigation uses the
desired track's Y axes\&. The native crosshair's vertical line spans all tracks and its horizontal line follows
the pointer's row\&.
.PP
Line and strip elements retain their existing -decimate none|auto option; the default remains none\&.
Set -decimate auto on each desired element\&. Density is measured along the shared physical X-axis span,
not by the number of tracks: plot width normally, plot height when inverted\&. Local Y mapping and clipping
still use the owning track\&. The existing eligibility rules apply, including fallback for smoothed traces
and unsupported fill/style combinations\&. Data-domain caches remain per element and follow vector updates;
closest-point queries retain original samples, and exports use full geometry\&. See \fI::rbc::LINEELEMENT\fP\&.
.PP
Each row also owns an optional local legend, hidden by default\&. For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g track legend voltage configure -hide no -position right -anchor ne
.fi
.RE
.PP
shows only that row's element labels\&. Local legends overlay and clip to the track; they do not change shared X
alignment\&. The global legend remains independent\&. See \fI::rbc::LEGEND\fP for options, bindings and export
behavior\&.
.SS "INVERTED TRACKS"
.PP
Set \&.g configure -invertxy yes to rotate the layout into side-by-side columns\&. Track order runs left to
right, shared X axes run vertically, and each track's Y axes run horizontally below its column\&. The common
bottom margin accommodates the largest set of track Y axes\&. Shared X grid lines are horizontal; local Y grid
lines are vertical\&. Track weights divide the available width\&. The existing -minheight option now requests a
minimum column width; it retains its name and value when toggling orientation\&. Tiny layouts compress those
minima proportionally in either orientation\&.
.PP
track identify and track sashpos take a physical X coordinate in inverted mode\&. track bbox, track
separator, track transform and track invtransform always use physical widget X/Y coordinates\&. Axis names
and data X/Y values retain their meaning\&. Markers, picking, local legends and exports follow the column
rectangle\&. Legend positions and anchors remain physical directions: right is still the right edge\&. Global and
local legends can be shown together, as in demos/trackgraph1\&.tcl\&. Toolbar navigation works in either
orientation; changing orientation during a gesture cancels it\&.
.SS "REORDERING AND RESIZING TRACKS"
.PP
track order takes the full list of existing track names, with no omissions or duplicates\&. Ownership, axis
configuration, vector subscriptions and marker bindings remain unchanged\&. Legend order still follows element
order, independently of track order\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g track order {current voltage}
set y [\&.g track sashpos current]
\&.g track sashpos current [expr {$y+30}]
\&.g configure -trackresize yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
track sashpos changes the two adjacent tracks' weights while preserving their sum\&. Other tracks keep their
weights and allocated space, subject to one-pixel layout rounding\&. Requested positions are clamped by the
adjacent minimum heights and the positive weight range\&. The returned position is the actual one\&. If minimum
heights already consume the available space, the separator cannot move\&. The resulting weights continue to
govern later window resizes and exports; no fixed pixel sizes are stored\&.
.PP
-trackresize is a graph option with database name trackResize, class TrackResize, and default no\&. Only
trackgraph widgets accept yes\&. With it enabled, the standard Trackgraph class bindings show a vertical
resize cursor over a row gap, or a horizontal cursor between inverted columns\&. Drag with button 1 to resize the
adjacent tracks; release to keep the result, or press Escape to restore the weights from the start of the
drag\&. The gesture borrows keyboard focus so Escape works, then restores the previous focus on release,
cancellation or invalidation, provided focus is still on the graph\&. If there was no previous focus or its
widget has been destroyed, focus returns to the graph's toplevel\&. Focus moved elsewhere during dragging is left
alone; a graph already focused before dragging remains focused\&.  Pointer events in plot interiors keep their
ordinary behaviour\&. Changing the track list/order, orientation, or disabling resizing invalidates the gesture
on the next motion or release, without restoring obsolete weights\&. Destroying the widget removes its gesture
state\&. These dependency-free bindings are loaded with package require rbc\&.
.PP
Optional divider lines make separator gaps visible\&. -trackseparatorcolor (database name
trackSeparatorColor, class TrackSeparatorColor) defaults to empty, disabling the lines\&.
-trackseparatorwidth (trackSeparatorWidth, TrackSeparatorWidth) defaults to 1 and accepts a positive Tk
screen distance\&. These graph options affect only trackgraphs\&. For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g configure -trackresize yes -trackseparatorcolor gray60 -trackseparatorwidth 2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each divider follows the centre returned by track sashpos, horizontally for rows and vertically for inverted
columns\&. Its width is limited to the available gap\&. It does not change track geometry or enlarge the existing
separator hit area, and can be shown without enabling dragging\&. Dividers appear in SVG, PDF and PostScript
exports when -decorations yes is selected\&.
.PP
Applications may call the native order and separator operations regardless of -trackresize; the option
controls only the supplied mouse bindings\&. Custom interaction code can leave it disabled and use the same
commands\&. Keep the Trackgraph class in bindtags to use the standard bindings; a widget binding that returns
break before the class binding can intentionally suppress them\&.
.PP
Trackgraph supports line and strip elements in both orientations\&. Bar/polar elements are rejected\&. Track
scrolling is not yet provided\&. The optional rbc::graphtoolbar package accepts -type trackgraph: zoom and
pan use shared X axes and the starting track's Y axes; wheel zoom and coordinate annotations follow the pointed
track\&. Separator dragging remains available\&. See \fI::rbc::graphtoolbar\fP and demos/trackgraph1\&.tcl for the
interaction controls\&.  Markers can select a track with -track name -mapy axisName; see \fI::rbc::MARKER\fP for
clipping, ownership and restrictions\&. See demos/trackgraph1\&.tcl for multiple shared X axes, live vector
updates, track weights, annotations and shared horizontal navigation\&.


